```

## Performance Analysis

**(i) Data Structure:** Maintain spanning forest in RAM, buffered edges on disk with locality-aware partitioning, and periodic rebuild of compact connectivity structure.

**(ii) Amortized I/O:** O((n + mb)/B) per batch, where rebuild occurs every T/b batches.

**(iii) Query Time:** O(log_B n) average case, O(m/B) worst case when searching residual edges.

**(iv) Space:** O(n + m + T) where T is buffer threshold, typically Θ(n + m).

**(v) Optimizations:** 1) Adaptive rebuild threshold based on update/query ratio; 2) Multi-level buffering with hot/cold edge classification.

**(vi) Comparison:** Recomputing from scratch costs O((n+m)α(n,m)/B) per batch vs. O((n+mb)/B) amortized with batching—significant improvement when b << m.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to combine streaming sketches with efficient top-K data structures while reasoning about accuracy, space, and time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Processing unbounded data with limited memory
- **Count-Min Sketch (CMS):** Probabilistic data structure for frequency estimation with false positives but no false negatives
- **Top-K Maintenance:** Using a min-heap to track the K most frequent elements efficiently
- **Amortized Analysis:** Bounding average cost over sequences of operations
- **Space-Time Trade-offs:** Comparing approximate vs. exact approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
class CountMinSketch {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w;
    this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => Array(w).fill(0));
  }

  hash(key, i) {
    const str = typeof key === 'string' ? key : String(key);
    let hash = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < str.length; j++) {
      hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(j)) >>> 0;
    }
    return (hash + i * 31) % this.w;
  }

  update(key) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      this.table[i][j]++;
    }
  }

  estimate(key) {
    let min = Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][j]);
    }
    return min;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design incremental single-source shortest paths with batch updates and to reason about amortized work and practical data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental SSSP with batch edge insertions
- Localized relaxation and pruning
- Priority queues and potential bucketed variants (Dial's)
- Amortized analysis and space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateBatch(batchEdges){
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    // ensure undirected view if needed
    adj[u].push([v,w]);
    adj[v].push([u,w]);
  }
  for (const [u,v,w] of batchEdges){
    if (dist[v] > dist[u] + w){
      dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
      pq.push([dist[v], v]);
    }
    if (dist[u] > dist[v] + w){
      dist[u] = dist[v] + w;
      pq.push([dist[u], u]);
    }
  }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, x] = pq.pop();
    if (d !== dist[x]) continue;
    for (const [y,w] of adj[x]){
      if (dist[y] > dist[x] + w){
        dist[y] = dist[x] + w;
        pq.push([dist[y], y]);
      }
    }
  }
}
```
